export const debate = {"id":"dennett-caruso-free-will-responsibility-2021","number":"185","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Daniel Dennett vs Gregg Caruso: Free Will and Moral Responsibility","label":"Compatibilism, desert, and punishment","date":"2026-08-21","duration":"2 hr 6 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AxhA7S3q49o","motion":"Does the natural human capacity for reasons-responsive control justify free will, moral desert, and responsibility practices?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Caruso’s carefully delimited desert skepticism and forward-looking alternatives outperform Dennett’s practical compatibilism, whose institutional strengths leave ultimate sourcehood and desert’s independent role unresolved.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"we have the rules and we have agreed to play by them","context":"Dennett distinguishes the consequentialist justification of a legal system from rule-bound adjudication within that established system."},"con":{"text":"future reconciliation future safety future moral formation","context":"Caruso identifies forward-looking purposes that responsibility practices can serve without relying on basic-desert responsibility."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Compatibilist free will is worth wanting","speaker":"Daniel Dennett","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Basic-desert free will is unjustified","speaker":"Gregg Caruso","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":79,"con":89},"sections":[{"sectionId":"section-control-and-alternatives","title":"Reasons-Responsive Control and the Ability to Do Otherwise","timebox":"13:31–73:26","score":{"pro":77,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-control-practical-changeability","time":"27:55","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Determinism does not prevent responsibility-relevant change: people can respond to persuasion, revise conduct, develop competence, and turn their lives around through reasons and reflection.","score":72,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply vividly separates determinism from fatalism by emphasizing observable persuasion, behavioral revision, moral development, and the continuing practical significance of reasons-responsive human capacities. Principal limitation: Change across altered reasons and circumstances does not answer whether agents ultimately author themselves or could settle alternatives under identical conditions, so the decisive skeptical qualification remains unaddressed. Live burden: The compatibilist route must explicitly connect conditional changeability to the particular control sufficient for desert, rather than treating practical flexibility as automatically resolving the sourcehood controversy. Locked score: The mixed band reflects strong relevance to natural self-control and accessible experiential support, offset by scope instability, overstated rhetoric, comparatively weak representational charity, and classification as a relevant nonanswer to both targeted objections.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-indeterminacy-settlement-gap","time":"13:31","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Adding indeterminacy at a strategically important point in deliberation does not provide desert-grounding control, because the agent still cannot settle which supported outcome ultimately occurs.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument precisely confines itself to event-causal libertarian accounts and identifies settlement, rather than mere unpredictability, as the responsibility-relevant deficiency requiring explanation. Principal limitation: Its compact presentation establishes a substantial conceptual objection but supplies neither sustained engagement with agent-causal alternatives nor an independent demonstration that settlement is necessary for basic desert. Live burden: The skeptical route must connect this bounded indeterministic shortfall to its broader sourcehood thesis while allowing that practical deliberation, reasons-responsiveness, and ordinary agency can remain consequentially significant. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects excellent central relevance, coherent qualification, and direct conceptual leverage, tempered by comparatively limited evidential development and minimal consideration of philosophically developed opposing models.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-control-ordinary-ability-otherwise","time":"49:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"An informed, undamaged, unrestrained, reasons-responsive person exercising adequate self-control meaningfully could have acted otherwise, even when the person’s action had deterministic causes.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply offers operationally intelligible conditions for ordinary alternative ability, including adequate information, freedom from restraint, unimpaired functioning, responsiveness to reasons, and effective self-control. Principal limitation: Defining “could have done otherwise” through these capacities largely stipulates the compatibilist interpretation and does not establish why conditional ability is sufficient for desert-based blame. Live burden: The argument must show that this everyday modal standard captures the responsibility-relevant dispute rather than changing the subject from ultimate settlement to counterfactual sensitivity across circumstances. Locked score: The strong band reflects precision, practical coherence, major direct contact, and a useful capacity-based account, while recognizing that the central normative sufficiency bridge remains incompletely defended against the skeptic’s accepted distinction.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-reasons-without-desert-freedom","time":"72:17","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Reasons-responsive freedom remains real and important under determinism because reasons alter beliefs and actions, but that practical control does not establish basic-desert moral responsibility.","score":94,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply fully grants the practical compatibilist insight that reasons can alter beliefs and actions, then precisely locates disagreement in whether this natural control entails basic desert. Principal limitation: The separation is analytically clear but still depends on the contested premise that desert requires something beyond reasons-responsive agency, whose exact content remains incompletely specified. Live burden: The skeptic must justify the additional sourcehood requirement without depreciating the genuine freedom, deliberation, moral learning, and interpersonal address already conceded to ordinary agents. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects exceptional coherence, motion-level relevance, representational charity, and direct responsiveness, with strong warrant from the causal role of reasons, tempered principally by the unresolved positive account of desert-grounding control.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-sourcehood-and-manipulation","title":"Sourcehood, Determinism, and Manipulation","timebox":"17:51–63:28","score":{"pro":82,"con":87},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-sourcehood-developed-self-control","time":"46:54","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Autonomous adults become largely self-made through sustained learning, reflection, practice, exposure to reasons, and moral education, notwithstanding the deterministic histories shaping those developmental processes.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply replaces instantaneous or ultimate authorship with a temporally extended account of self-formation involving learning, practice, reflection, moral education, exposure to reasons, and recognized impairment exceptions. Principal limitation: Calling mature agents “largely self-made” does not explain why causally inherited capacities and environments become desert-conferring authorship, so the skeptic’s regress concerning formative conditions persists. Live burden: The compatibilist must defend practical, historical sourcehood as normatively sufficient without implying impossible self-creation, and must identify when development genuinely belongs to the agent rather than manipulation or malfunction. Locked score: The strong band recognizes a coherent developmental mechanism, substantial direct responsiveness, clear burden contact, and reasonable charity, while preserving the material bridge problem between acquired autonomy and backward-looking desert.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-ultimate-sourcehood-denial","time":"17:51","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Because agents’ characters and actions ultimately result from factors beyond their control, they lack moral responsibility in the specifically identified basic-desert sense under determinism or indeterminism.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The thesis directly states the skeptical route’s motion-level foundation and carefully restricts its denial to pervasive basic-desert responsibility rather than indiscriminately rejecting agency or accountability. Principal limitation: The categorical transition from ultimately uncontrolled constitutive factors to absent desert receives compressed justification, leaving the necessity of ultimate sourcehood substantially asserted rather than independently established. Live burden: The case must explain why developed reasons-responsive self-government cannot provide sufficient authorship, while preserving the explicitly acknowledged causal, attributive, communicative, and forward-looking forms of responsibility. Locked score: The strong band recognizes exceptional burden relevance, stable terminology, and an intelligible sourcehood principle, while registering thinner argumentative support, limited comparison with compatibilist sufficiency standards, and somewhat overstated conclusiveness.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-sourcehood-interpersonal-interference","time":"62:35","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Manipulation undermines responsibility when another person deliberately displaces the agent’s control, whereas ordinary deterministic causation alone does not constitute that responsibility-undermining interpersonal interference.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies deliberate interpersonal displacement as a morally salient feature distinguishing manipulation from ordinary deterministic development, thereby resisting an indiscriminate inference from causation to compromised agency. Principal limitation: The distinction explains why manipulation is objectionable but does not establish that noninterpersonal causal histories confer sufficient sourcehood, especially when engineered and ordinary histories produce identical internal capacities. Live burden: The compatibilist must clarify whether historical authenticity, present reasons-responsiveness, or absence of another controller grounds responsibility and explain how those criteria handle gradual manipulation cases. Locked score: The strong band reflects a relevant principled distinction, substantial direct responsiveness, and useful attention to displaced control, while retaining unresolved equivalence cases and an incomplete bridge from nonmanipulation to desert.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-agent-control-sufficiency-gap","time":"55:44","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Compatibilist agent control may distinguish ordinary agency from manipulation or malfunction, yet those distinctions do not establish the further control required for basic-desert responsibility.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The challenge grants compatibilist agent control substantial practical importance while isolating the disputed inference from reasons-responsive ownership to the stronger conclusion of basic-desert responsibility. Principal limitation: The proposed additional control requirement remains comparatively abstract, and the move does not fully specify what non-question-begging condition could satisfy it or why satisfaction is independently necessary. Live burden: The skeptic must demonstrate that manipulation and malfunction reveal a principled insufficiency within ordinary agency, rather than merely contrasting normal causal development with responsibility-undermining interpersonal interference. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects excellent diagnostic precision, charitable concession, central burden relevance, and direct exposure of a sufficiency gap, tempered by the continuing need to articulate a positive desert condition.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-desert-conceptions","title":"Basic Desert and Ordinary Compatibilist Desert","timebox":"30:20–108:12","score":{"pro":76,"con":91},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-desert-sufficient-ordinary-responsibility","time":"34:37","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Basic desert is the wrong threshold; sufficient ordinary moral responsibility can instead justify proportionate coercion, imprisonment, fines, and reputational consequences for responsible legal violations.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly contests the skeptic’s required threshold and insists that ordinary responsibility, rather than ultimate self-creation, is the normatively relevant basis for lawful consequences. Principal limitation: The phrase “enough moral responsibility” lacks a developed sufficiency criterion, and permissible coercion is asserted without independently deriving proportionality, imprisonment, or desert from reasons-responsive competence. Live burden: The compatibilist case must distinguish its justification from the skeptic’s public-protection rationale and identify exactly what ordinary desert contributes beyond causal attribution, deterrence, rehabilitation, and prevention. Locked score: The strong band reflects immediate motion contact and meaningful responsiveness, but its lower boundary records unstable terminology, materially overstated entitlement, limited warrant, and incomplete engagement with the strongest non-desert alternative.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-basic-desert-limited-target","time":"30:20","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Free-will skepticism rejects basic-desert responsibility but preserves causal attribution and forward-looking moral engagement grounded in future reconciliation, public safety, and moral formation.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply sharply delimits skepticism, preserves legitimate accountability functions, and supplies reconciliation, safety, and moral formation as explicit non-desert foundations rather than merely denying compatibilist claims. Principal limitation: These enumerated purposes establish a credible framework but do not alone demonstrate that every defensible blaming, sanctioning, or communicative practice can be reconstructed without residual desert commitments. Live burden: The skeptical route must show how its alternatives retain proportionality, moral address, institutional stability, and interpersonal seriousness while avoiding the backward-looking justification it rejects. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects outstanding motion relevance, disciplined qualification, exceptional charity, and a successful justified reframe, with only bounded evidential incompleteness concerning implementation and comparative institutional performance.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-desert-everyday-conduct-sensitive","time":"83:26","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Basic desert demands something nonexistent, whereas familiar everyday deservingness tracks conduct and comparative performance and remains intelligible within a deterministically caused world.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply clearly separates basic desert from familiar conduct-sensitive deservingness and supplies an accessible better-team example illustrating ordinary comparative usage under deterministic conditions. Principal limitation: Demonstrating familiar linguistic practice does not establish that everyday deservingness justifies moral condemnation, deprivation, imprisonment, or any backward-looking institutional consequence beyond evaluative recognition. Live burden: The compatibilist must articulate the normative content of ordinary desert and connect it to proportionate responsibility practices without relying merely on familiarity or equivocating between achievement and punishment. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct motion relevance, a recognizable conceptual alternative, and responsive engagement, while its placement records modest logical and evidential support, incomplete characterization of basic desert, and the unresolved transition from ordinary usage to coercive justification.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-desert-dispensability-dilemma","time":"105:22","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"If backward-looking desert independently justifies punishment, the theory is mixed rather than purely consequentialist; if desert contributes no justification, it is dispensable and should be removed.","score":90,"critique":"Strongest feature: The diagnostic states a crisp dilemma: independently justificatory desert makes the account mixed, whereas nonjustificatory desert becomes dispensable, directly challenging the coherence of consequentialist desert. Principal limitation: Its two branches compress possible derivative, constitutive, expressive, or rule-internal roles through which desert terminology might operate within an ultimately consequentialist institutional design. Live burden: The skeptic must demonstrate that these intermediate roles either covertly introduce backward-looking justification or contribute nothing unavailable through clearer forward-looking concepts, institutional rules, and practical arrangements. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, logical clarity, precise targeting, substantial charity, and major leverage against the opposing institutional account, while retaining only a bounded gap concerning sophisticated rule-consequentialist constructions of desert.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-sanctions-and-public-protection","title":"Punishment, Public Safety, and Institutional Justification","timebox":"36:51–111:41","score":{"pro":85,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-sanctions-public-proportionate-law","time":"73:35","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Public, proportionate punishment can preserve respect for valuable legal institutions and instantiate consequentialist desert, regardless of whether human action is deterministically or indeterministically caused.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply connects responsibility practices to publicly known, proportionate legal sanctions and grounds their preservation in institutional consequences rather than dependence on a particular metaphysics of causation. Principal limitation: Calling the resulting account “consequentialist desert” leaves desert’s independent contribution unclear, while the claimed superiority of punishment over less restrictive alternatives receives insufficient comparative support. Live burden: The compatibilist must explain why respected rules require deserved punishment specifically, rather than proportionate prevention, restitution, rehabilitation, or deterrence administered without backward-looking desert. Locked score: The strong band recognizes complete motion contact, institutional concreteness, stable public-law considerations, and meaningful responsiveness, while recording conceptual instability, limited evidence, incomplete charity toward least-restrictive alternatives, and an unresolved dispensability problem.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-quarantine-public-protection","time":"36:51","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Significant public danger can justify proportionate, least-restrictive liberty limitations through self-defense and harm prevention, without invoking free will, retribution, or basic desert.","score":91,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply provides a concrete non-desert justification for coercion and expressly constrains governmental authority through significant danger, proportionality, self-defense, harm prevention, and least-restrictive intervention. Principal limitation: The quarantine analogy leaves morally important differences between disease transmission and intentional criminal conduct underdeveloped, including communication, equal treatment, evidentiary standards, and agency-sensitive legal classification. Live burden: The alternative framework must demonstrate that calibrated prevention and rehabilitation can govern actual institutions without collapsing persons into predicted risks or disregarding legitimate distinctions among offenses. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects complete motion contact, careful qualification, strong coherence, substantial evidential warrant, and charitable responsiveness, moderated only by bounded analogical and implementation gaps.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-sanctions-rule-governed-consequentialism","time":"109:08","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Consequentialist reasons may justify a stable punishment system even though adjudicators properly apply agreed public rules instead of recalculating every individual punishment’s expected consequences.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply clearly distinguishes system-level consequentialist justification from case-level rule application and explains how repeated individualized recalculation could undermine predictability, legality, and institutional confidence. Principal limitation: The distinction successfully defends rule governance but does not identify desert’s independent role, and categorical disregard of case consequences risks excluding justified exceptions and safeguards. Live burden: The compatibilist must explain how public rules incorporate proportionality, error correction, changed circumstances, and least-restrictive treatment while retaining a genuinely consequentialist foundation. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects excellent responsiveness, coherent institutional architecture, strong central relevance, and a concrete rule-versus-act distinction, moderated by limited empirical warrant and the surviving challenge that stable punishment rules need not employ desert terminology.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-risk-sensitive-legal-distinctions","time":"64:30","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A forward-looking legal framework can preserve distinctions among offenses by considering conduct, evidence, danger, rehabilitation, proportionality, and individualized risk without treating punishment as deserved.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply shows that rejecting desert need not erase offense-sensitive legal distinctions, because evidence, conduct, danger, rehabilitation prospects, proportionality, and individualized risk can support differentiated treatment. Principal limitation: Heavy reliance on prospective risk introduces familiar accuracy, equality, due-process, and preventive-detention concerns that the concise presentation does not systematically constrain or institutionally resolve. Live burden: The skeptical framework must specify evidentiary thresholds, review procedures, maximum restrictions, and safeguards against discriminatory prediction while retaining meaningful recognition of completed wrongdoing. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects direct responsiveness, practical specificity, strong relevance to alternative institutions, and successful resistance to an undifferentiated-treatment objection, moderated by significant but bounded implementation risks surrounding individualized prediction.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"section-reactive-attitudes-and-reform","title":"Reactive Attitudes and Reform of Responsibility Practices","timebox":"98:55–125:43","score":{"pro":74,"con":82},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-reactive-moral-anger-adult-wrongdoing","time":"124:02","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Disappointment may guide children, but terrible wrongdoing by competent adults can warrant moral anger that publicly communicates seriousness and discourages comparable misconduct by others.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly distinguishes developmental guidance from responses to competent adult wrongdoing and identifies moral anger’s possible condemnatory and public-warning functions in exceptionally serious cases. Principal limitation: The asserted entitlement to anger rests mainly on an intuitive political example and does not show why firm protest, sorrow, disappointment, or sanctions cannot communicate equivalent seriousness. Live burden: The compatibilist must provide a fittingness standard for moral anger, distinguish it from hostility or retribution, and demonstrate benefits unavailable through non-desert reactive attitudes. Locked score: The mixed band reflects immediate contact with the decisive replacement challenge and a recognizable adult-wrongdoing distinction, offset by materially overstated certainty, weak evidential warrant, incomplete charity, and failure to establish functional uniqueness.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evolved-practices-revisable","time":"98:55","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Responsibility practices have changed historically, including movement beyond honor cultures, so society can further reform them by relinquishing potentially harmful concepts of just desert and retribution.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply uses historical movement from honor cultures toward individualistic practices to establish that responsibility conventions are socially evolved, revisable, and not fixed by psychological familiarity. Principal limitation: Past change demonstrates reform’s possibility but not the superiority, feasibility, or harmlessness of abandoning desert, and the asserted dangers of retained concepts receive limited evidential support. Live burden: The reformist case must compare concrete replacement practices with existing institutions and show that they preserve condemnation, deterrence, moral communication, fairness, and protection across serious cases. Locked score: The strong band reflects careful modal calibration, a relevant historical example, charitable recognition of continuing functions, and effective subsidiary burden contact, tempered by limited evidence for harmfulness and replacement effectiveness.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-replacement-reactive-attitudes","time":"121:37","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Desert-dependent moral anger may be replaced by disappointment, sorrow, and related non-desert responses that preserve, and perhaps improve, interpersonal correction and responsibility practices.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply supplies concrete replacement attitudes, rather than merely recommending emotional restraint, and connects them to preserved interpersonal correction without presupposing basic desert. Principal limitation: A parental example cannot establish that disappointment or sorrow performs exactly the same communicative, motivational, protective, and condemnatory work in serious adult wrongdoing. Live burden: The reformist case must test alternative attitudes across betrayal, violence, exploitation, institutional misconduct, and repeated offenses while distinguishing moral protest from hostile desert-based anger. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct functional engagement, stable qualification, intelligible alternatives, and meaningful reform relevance, while registering limited comparative evidence, an overextended equivalence claim, and incomplete representation of the opponent’s strongest adult-wrongdoing cases.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":79,"strengths":["Developed a concrete compatibilist account of agency through learning, reflection, moral education, reasons-responsiveness, and unimpaired self-control.","Distinguished deliberate interpersonal manipulation from ordinary deterministic causation by focusing on whether another person displaces the agent’s control.","Offered a coherent system-versus-act distinction explaining how consequentialist institutional design can coexist with rule-bound adjudication.","Connected sanctions to publicity, proportionality, legal stability, and impairment-sensitive exceptions rather than to metaphysical indeterminism."],"blunders":[{"text":"Practical changeability answered fatalism more effectively than the skeptic’s distinct demands for ultimate sourcehood and outcome settlement.","links":[]},{"text":"The account repeatedly invoked ordinary or consequentialist desert without clearly identifying what desert adds beyond forward-looking institutional benefits.","links":[]},{"text":"The defense of moral anger relied on an intuitive adult-wrongdoing example without establishing a fittingness standard or functional advantage over alternatives.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":89,"strengths":["Carefully limited skepticism to basic-desert responsibility while preserving causal attribution, deliberation, moral engagement, and practical freedom.","Supplied substantive non-desert grounds for coercion through self-defense, public safety, proportionality, rehabilitation, and least-restrictive intervention.","Granted the practical force of reasons-responsive agency and precisely isolated the remaining dispute over desert-grounding control.","Pressed a powerful dispensability dilemma against backward-looking desert within an account presented as consequentialist."],"blunders":[{"text":"The sourcehood case did not fully specify a positive, non-question-begging condition for the additional control that basic desert would require.","links":[]},{"text":"The quarantine analogy and risk-sensitive framework left important due-process, equality, prediction, and offense-recognition safeguards underdeveloped.","links":[]},{"text":"The proposed replacement attitudes were supported mainly through a parental example and did not establish equivalent performance in serious adult cases.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that mature, reasons-responsive self-government can justify bounded ordinary responsibility and proportionate accountability without requiring ultimate self-creation or metaphysically open alternatives.","premises":["Responsibility-relevant control consists in stable capacities to understand reasons, foresee consequences, revise conduct, govern impulses, and act without coercion, manipulation, or disabling impairment.","These capacities are genuinely attributable to an adult when they persist across time, integrate reflection and moral learning, and are not bypassed by another controller.","Ordinary desert should mean conduct-sensitive eligibility for proportionate responses under justified public rules, not suffering valuable independently of social purposes.","A legitimate sanctioning system requires prospective justification, public notice, proportionality, due process, impairment defenses, review mechanisms, and humane limits on every imposed burden.","Within such a system, backward-looking facts about voluntary conduct can guide rule application because they identify when an agent’s protected capacities actually governed the violation."],"conclusion":"Therefore, determinism need not defeat ordinary responsibility: suitably competent agents may be held answerable through humane, proportionate, publicly justified practices tied to their exercised capacities and conduct."},"newArguments":[{"title":"The social assurance argument","text":"Accountability rules can function as reciprocal assurances among citizens who possess ordinary self-control. Each person accepts predictable constraints because others are likewise required to answer for voluntary violations, while incapacity and coercion remain defenses. This does not treat suffering as intrinsically good. It explains why conduct-sensitive responsibility can add something to generic risk management: it publicly recognizes whether a person could understand and comply with shared terms, thereby supporting equal civic standing and stable cooperation."},{"title":"The institutional anti-bypass principle","text":"A rule-governed system should attribute responsibility only when the agent’s deliberative capacities were part of the action-producing pathway. Manipulation, coercion, severe misinformation, and impairment reduce responsibility because they bypass or disable those capacities; deterministic causation by itself does neither. This principle converts the manipulation distinction into an administrable test and avoids claiming that agents created their own constitutions. It also explains graduated responsibility by asking how completely competent self-government participated in the specific violation."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that practical agency warrants engagement and protection, but no demonstrated feature of natural self-control independently justifies basic-desert blame or suffering.","premises":["Reasons-responsive agents deliberate, learn, and modify behavior, yet their constitutive capacities and histories arise through conditions they did not ultimately select.","Adding indeterminacy cannot repair this limitation unless it supplies controlled settlement rather than introducing chance among outcomes supported by deliberation.","Protection, deterrence, rehabilitation, restitution, and moral formation can justify proportionate responses without treating suffering as deserved independently of their defensible purposes.","Forward-looking restrictions must satisfy demanding safeguards: reliable evidence, individualized necessity, equal protection, periodic review, humane conditions, and the least restrictive effective intervention.","Moral protest can communicate seriousness, recognize victims, demand repair, and set boundaries without adding the claim that an offender basically deserves hostility or suffering."],"conclusion":"Therefore, society may preserve answerability, moral address, restitution, and necessary protection while withholding basic-desert condemnation and continuously reviewing coercive practices for necessity and fairness."},"newArguments":[{"title":"The error-cost asymmetry argument","text":"Desert-based punishment can amplify institutional error because a mistaken judgment appears to authorize suffering as fitting, not merely as an unfortunate protective measure. A non-desert framework instead treats every restriction as a regrettable burden requiring continuing justification. That orientation supports review, correction, compensation, and early release when risk or evidence changes. Because criminal institutions predictably make mistakes, choosing the framework that keeps burdens presumptively contestable provides an independent reason to reject basic-desert justification."},{"title":"Plural replacement for moral anger","text":"No single emotion must reproduce every function attributed to moral anger. A coordinated repertoire can divide the labor: moral protest communicates condemnation, sorrow registers harm, protective resolve sets boundaries, restitution addresses victims, and sanctions prevent recurrence. This plural model answers the adult-wrongdoing objection more effectively than substituting disappointment alone. Its success should be assessed by communication, victim recognition, deterrence, reform, and relational repair, allowing empirical comparison without presupposing that hostile anger is uniquely fitting."}]}},"year":2021};
